{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"PMCID:PMC3123044","internalQuery":"PMCID:PMC3123044","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":1,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"21540185","source":"MED","pmid":"21540185","pmcid":"PMC3123044","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC3123044"]},"doi":"10.1074/jbc.m111.231316","title":"The acylation state of surface lipoproteins of mollicute Acholeplasma laidlawii.","authorString":"Serebryakova MV, Demina IA, Galyamina MA, Kondratov IG, Ladygina VG, Govorun VM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Serebryakova MV","lastName":"Serebryakova","firstName":"Marina V","initials":"MV","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5402-8215"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Physico-Chemical Medicine, Federal Medico-Biological Agency, Moscow 119992, Russia. mserebr@mail.ru"}]}},{"fullName":"Demina IA","lastName":"Demina","firstName":"Irina A","initials":"IA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4317-2094"}},{"fullName":"Galyamina MA","lastName":"Galyamina","firstName":"Maria A","initials":"MA"},{"fullName":"Kondratov IG","lastName":"Kondratov","firstName":"Ilya G","initials":"IG"},{"fullName":"Ladygina VG","lastName":"Ladygina","firstName":"Valentina G","initials":"VG"},{"fullName":"Govorun VM","lastName":"Govorun","firstName":"Vadim M","initials":"VM"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4317-2094"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5402-8215"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"26","volume":"286","journalIssueId":1843211,"dateOfPublication":"2011 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2011,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Biol Chem","title":"The Journal of biological chemistry","essn":"1083-351X","issn":"0021-9258","isoabbreviation":"J Biol Chem","nlmid":"2985121R"},"printPublicationDate":"2011-07-01"},"pubYear":"2011","pageInfo":"22769-22776","abstractText":"Acylation of the N-terminal Cys residue is an essential, ubiquitous, and uniquely bacterial posttranslational modification that allows anchoring of proteins to the lipid membrane. In gram-negative bacteria, acylation proceeds through three sequential steps requiring lipoprotein diacylglyceryltransferase, lipoprotein signal peptidase, and finally lipoprotein N-acyltransferase. The apparent lack of genes coding for recognizable homologs of lipoprotein N-acyltransferase in gram-positive bacteria and Mollicutes suggests that the final step of the protein acylation process may be absent in these organisms. In this work, we monitored the acylation state of eight major lipoproteins of the mollicute Acholeplasma laidlawii using a combination of standard two-dimensional gel electrophoresis protein separation, blotting to nitrocellulose membranes, and MALDI-MS identification of modified N-terminal tryptic peptides. We show that for each A. laidlawii lipoprotein studied a third fatty acid in an amide linkage on the N-terminal Cys residue is present, whereas diacylated species were not detected. The result thus proves that A. laidlawii encodes a lipoprotein N-acyltransferase activity. We hypothesize that N-acyltransferases encoded by genes non-homologous to N-acyltransferases of gram-negative bacteria are also present in other mollicutes and gram-positive bacteria.","affiliation":"Institute of Physico-Chemical Medicine, Federal Medico-Biological Agency, Moscow 119992, Russia. mserebr@mail.ru","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Acholeplasma laidlawii","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbr